1. Autoregressive Distributed Lag (ARDL) Dynamic Framework
Conceptual and Econometric Justification
The Autoregressive Distributed Lag (ARDL) framework is deployed to model the complex, time-varying transmission mechanisms of the South African economy. Standard macro-econometric models often impose restrictive assumptions, forcing all exogenous shocks to impact GDP instantaneously or with uniform delays. The ARDL framework relaxes this constraint, allowing each specific macroeconomic indicator to transmit through the economy at its own unique empirical speed. Furthermore, the ARDL structure inherently addresses potential endogeneity issues and provides highly robust estimates even in small, finite samples—a critical advantage when dealing with quarterly GDP data.
