2. Mixed Data Sampling with LASSO Regularization (LASSO-MIDAS)
Conceptual and Econometric Justification
The fundamental bottleneck in macroeconomic nowcasting is the "ragged edge" problem combined with frequency mismatch. Quarterly GDP is released with a significant lag, whereas leading indicators (PMI, vehicle sales, money supply) are published at varying high frequencies (monthly or weekly). Traditional OLS models require aggregating this high-frequency data into quarterly averages, fundamentally destroying critical intra-quarter timing signals. The Mixed Data Sampling (MIDAS) framework bridges this gap by mapping high-frequency predictors directly onto a low-frequency target, while the LASSO penalty acts as an algorithmic filter to ruthlessly eliminate uninformative noise.
Mathematical Framework & LASSO Integration
To prevent parameter proliferation—a common issue when introducing multiple high-frequency lags—this framework introduces two layers of regularization. First, it employs a parameterized weighting function (an exponential decay polynomial) to enforce the economic intuition that recent shocks impact the economy more severely than older shocks. Second, it applies an L_1 penalty (LASSO) to handle highly correlated macroeconomic indicators (multicollinearity) and select only the most robust signals.
